{
  "image_name": "646f1343926d026c48f97b7a236255c8.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares several RGB pixel architectures for OLED microdisplays and highlights how color is generated, patterned, and extracted. A conventional approach places red, green, and blue color filters above a white OLED stack built on Si-CMOS circuitry, with the usual anode, cathode, and reflective metal layers underneath. An alternative structure integrates separate red, green, and blue emitting regions directly into the OLED stack, using charge transport and injection layers to form color without relying only on filters.\n\nAnother panel illustrates pixel definition through shadow-mask deposition: material from a source passes through a fine metal mask to create distinct subpixels, but the drawing emphasizes a visible “shadow effect” that can blur boundaries and limit precise pixel formation. The figure also contrasts a single-stack white OLED with a more complex three-stack tandem white OLED, where multiple red, green, and blue emissive units are separated by charge-generation layers to boost emission.\n\nThe bottom row focuses on light extraction. One design uses microlenses formed on top of the color-filter side, while another places microlenses under a low-refractive-index layer above the glass. In both cases, the arrows indicate improved upward light output from the OLED through the display stack.",
